THERE ARE 5 TYPES OF HEAP PROBLEMS ON LEETCODE
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

TYPE 1 — TOP K ELEMENTS → keep a heap of size k
TYPE 2 — MERGE K SORTED → merge with a min-heap
TYPE 3 — TWO HEAPS → balance a max-heap and a min-heap
TYPE 4 — SCHEDULING / GREEDY → always process the best next task
TYPE 5 — LAZY DELETION → heap + hashmap to skip stale entries

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
TYPE 1 — TOP K ELEMENTS
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

When to use:
→ Find the k largest / k smallest / k most frequent elements
→ Find the k-th largest / k-th smallest element

Keywords: "k largest", "k smallest", "k most frequent", "k-th largest"

KEY INSIGHT:
To find the k LARGEST → maintain a MIN-heap of size k.
The top of the heap is always the smallest of the k largest.
If a new element is bigger than the top → pop the top, push the new one.
At the end, the heap contains the k largest elements.

To find the k SMALLEST → maintain a MAX-heap of size k.
Same idea reversed.

TEMPLATE — K Largest (min-heap of size k):

priority_queue<int, vector<int>, greater<int>> minH;  // min-heap

for (int x : nums) {
    minH.push(x);
    if (minH.size() > k)
        minH.pop();   // remove the smallest so only k largest remain
}

// minH.top() = k-th largest
// minH contains the k largest elements
TEMPLATE — K Smallest (max-heap of size k):

priority_queue<int> maxH;  // max-heap

for (int x : nums) {
    maxH.push(x);
    if (maxH.size() > k)
        maxH.pop();   // remove the largest so only k smallest remain
}

// maxH.top() = k-th smallest
// maxH contains the k smallest elements
EXAMPLE — Kth Largest Element in an Array
https://leetcode.com/problems/kth-largest-element-in-an-array

Maintain a min-heap of size k.
After processing all elements, top() = k-th largest.

int findKthLargest(vector<int>& nums, int k) {
    priority_queue<int, vector<int>, greater<int>> minH;
    for (int x : nums) {
        minH.push(x);
        if (minH.size() > k) minH.pop();
    }
    return minH.top();
}
EXAMPLE — K Closest Points to Origin
https://leetcode.com/problems/k-closest-points-to-origin

Distance = x² + y² (no need for sqrt).
Maintain a MAX-heap of size k on distance.
Kick out the farthest point whenever size exceeds k.

vector<vector<int>> kClosest(vector<vector<int>>& points, int k) {
    // {distance, index} — max-heap
    priority_queue<pair<int,int>> maxH;
    for (int i = 0; i < points.size(); i++) {
        int d = points[i][0]*points[i][0] + points[i][1]*points[i][1];
        maxH.push({d, i});
        if (maxH.size() > k) maxH.pop();
    }
    vector<vector<int>> res;
    while (!maxH.empty()) {
        res.push_back(points[maxH.top().second]);
        maxH.pop();
    }
    return res;
}
EXAMPLE — Top K Frequent Elements
https://leetcode.com/problems/top-k-frequent-elements

Count frequencies with a hashmap.
Run the "k largest" pattern on frequencies.

vector<int> topKFrequent(vector<int>& nums, int k) {
    unordered_map<int,int> freq;
    for (int x : nums) freq[x]++;

    // min-heap on {frequency, value}
    priority_queue<pair<int,int>, vector<pair<int,int>>, greater<>> minH;
    for (auto& [val, cnt] : freq) {
        minH.push({cnt, val});
        if (minH.size() > k) minH.pop();
    }
    vector<int> res;
    while (!minH.empty()) { res.push_back(minH.top().second); minH.pop(); }
    return res;
}
