TYPE 4 — SCHEDULING / GREEDY WITH A HEAP
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

When to use:
→ Assign tasks to workers to minimize time
→ Problems where you always want to process the "most urgent" or "least loaded" next
→ Job scheduling, meeting rooms, CPU task simulation

Keywords: "minimum time", "assign tasks", "k workers", "cooldown", "idle"

KEY INSIGHT:
Always pick the best available option greedily.
A heap gives you the best option in O(log n).

EXAMPLE — Task Scheduler
https://leetcode.com/problems/task-scheduler

You must wait n intervals between two of the same task.
Strategy: always execute the most frequent available task first.

int leastInterval(vector<char>& tasks, int n) {
    unordered_map<char,int> freq;
    for (char c : tasks) freq[c]++;

    priority_queue<int> maxH;  // max-heap on frequencies
    for (auto& [c, f] : freq) maxH.push(f);

    // Min-heap of {available_time, frequency} for cooling-down tasks
    priority_queue<pair<int,int>, vector<pair<int,int>>, greater<>> cooldown;

    int time = 0;
    while (!maxH.empty() || !cooldown.empty()) {
        time++;
        if (!maxH.empty()) {
            int f = maxH.top() - 1; maxH.pop();
            if (f > 0) cooldown.push({time + n, f});
        }
        if (!cooldown.empty() && cooldown.top().first == time) {
            maxH.push(cooldown.top().second); cooldown.pop();
        }
    }
    return time;
}
