COMMON MISTAKES AND HOW TO AVOID THEM
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

MISTAKE 1 — Using a max-heap when you need a min-heap and vice versa
To keep the k LARGEST, use a MIN-heap (so you can kick out the smallest quickly).
To keep the k SMALLEST, use a MAX-heap.
It's counterintuitive but that is how it works.

MISTAKE 2 — Forgetting to check empty() before calling top()
Calling top() or pop() on an empty heap is undefined behavior.
Always check empty() first.

MISTAKE 3 — Using a heap for sliding window maximum
A monotonic deque is O(n). A heap is O(n log n) with lazy deletion.
If the window is fixed size and you only need the max, use a deque.

MISTAKE 4 — Not rebalancing in the Two Heaps pattern
After every insert, you must ensure the sizes differ by at most 1.
Skipping the rebalance causes wrong medians.

MISTAKE 5 — Comparing floats in heap for median
Store as int. Only divide when returning the median.
Avoid floating point inside the heap comparison.

